    "html": "<p>The landing page now says it plainly — a <strong>Beta</strong> badge rides the header wordmark and the hero, with one sentence under the subhead: <em>&quot;expect rough edges and moving formats; report what breaks.&quot;</em> This post is the longhand version of that badge: what &quot;beta&quot; means for Unown specifically, why we label it at all, and what you can hold us to while it lasts.</p>\n<h2>What beta means here</h2>\n<p>Unown is open source, MIT licensed, free, and ungated — there is no waitlist, no seat count, no &quot;contact sales&quot; between you and the binary. So &quot;beta&quot; here is not a scarcity signal; it is an <strong>accuracy</strong> signal, and it makes three specific promises:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The release line is 0.0.x.</strong> Fifteen patch releases shipped in October alone. Patch-order versioning is honest about maturity: the version number itself says &quot;the surface is still settling.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Formats move.</strong> The changelog records format changes under release after release — the note format gained fields, the session export changed shape, memory rendering was re-platformed. Anything you build against Unown&#39;s files or output should pin a version and read the changelog before upgrading.</li>\n<li><strong>Rough edges are expected and wanted.</strong> The issue tracker is the reporting lane. Every report becomes a session note for the engine&#39;s harness on our side — the project&#39;s whole mechanism is turning what happened into remembered, verified practice.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What beta does NOT mean</h2>\n<p>It does not mean the core is speculative. The parts the landing page argues are the parts with receipts: the four-tier memory stack is files on disk you can <code>cat</code>, the consolidation pipeline is measured in committed benchmark receipts, and every quoted engine literal on the landing page is gated against the source that writes it. Beta means <strong>the seams move</strong>, not that the floor is soft.</p>\n<p>It also does not mean &quot;preview&quot; in the softened corporate sense. We chose the plain word on purpose: the product is open and ungated, so the accurate label for &quot;shipped, usable, still changing&quot; is beta — not &quot;early access&quot; (there is no gate) and not &quot;preview&quot; (nothing is being previewed; it is running).</p>\n<h2>What you can hold us to</h2>\n<p>While the badge is up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Every release keeps a changelog entry</strong> — the landing page&#39;s shipping rail is generated from <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>, so a release that skipped its entry fails CI, not just etiquette.</li>\n<li><strong>Every measured claim keeps its receipt</strong> — benchmark figures without a committed receipt are printed as &quot;not measured&quot;, never estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory stays inspectable</strong> — the terminal agent never opens a database; project memory is a markdown file in your repo, versioned and diffable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When the badge comes off</h2>\n<p>Graduation is not a date on a calendar — it is a state of the seams. The 0.0.x line grows up when the formats that external tools might depend on (the note format, the session export, the print-mode JSON envelope) have each survived a stretch of releases without a breaking change. The changelog will say so when it happens, and this post will get a <code>lastmod</code> bump.</p>\n<p>Until then: use it, break it, report it. The badge is a contract, and the issue tracker is where it is enforced.</p>\n"
